{"kind":"request","text":"Claude: give gs merge a supported closer for an approved head that the target already contains.\n\nProblem. Workrooms that are also updated directly, by local actors or by remote work, get out of sync in a way gs merge does not accommodate. It is worst when gitseq work targets a non-main branch that then goes through pull-request review and merge, or is pushed to directly: the approved head lands out of band, no receipt is sealed, and the lanes that the receipt would have closed stay open. When the implementer then runs gs merge, the plan refuses with \"approved candidate is already contained in the target\" (internal/mergeplan/mergeplan.go, the candidate check; docs/reference/gs/merge.md refusal table). Every other door is shut too. A plain report on a landing request is ineffective under the landing model. Supersede means withdrawn or carried, which would falsify the record. So the row sits in awaiting-landing with approved_not_landed set forever.\n\nAn agent in another project reached exactly this point and stopped, correctly, rather than write ten misleading supersedes. Its notes: \"The board is out of sync because the branch was landed out-of-band around the workroom; the receipts that would have closed these lanes were never created, and the tool that creates them now declines because Git already moved. The correct closer is a witnessed receipt for the merge that actually carried the work, which is builder's act, and which gs merge won't retroactively synthesize for an already-contained head. That's a gitseq mechanism gap.\"\n\nOutcome wanted. The most streamlined experience for the actor in that position: they run the same gs merge they would have run, with the same candidate, approval and checkout, and it records the truth instead of refusing. When the approved candidate is already contained in the checkout's target ref, and every other merge check passes, gs merge seals a durable incorporation receipt: the ordinary merge receipt with merge_incorporation=prior and an empty succession plan, merge_head naming the candidate and merge_target_pre_head the observed target head. Git is not touched. The fold closes the implementation commitment from that receipt exactly as it would from an ordinary landing, so the row projects satisfied and landed and approved_not_landed clears. No new flag, no new command, no new kind, no requester ratification.\n\nDesign basis. notes/2026-08-31-merged-head-commitment-reconciliation.md (live artifact at 296e0fdd3) is the reviewed candidate design for this closer and its authority argument stands: reachability is evidence, a signed receipt is the closure, and a prior receipt confers no retirement authority. This request adopts that argument and licenses two simplifications the note left open. First, detection replaces the note's --incorporated flag: the containment check already runs, so the command branches on its answer and says so on stderr. Second, no off-target Git receipt object or reference transaction is needed when the incorporation is one durable act with nothing to resume: single use is the existing durable check that refuses an approval that already has a receipt, and the resident's idempotency key makes a concurrent retry a replay. If you find that the durable log alone cannot give single use, say so in an assert on your promise and follow the note's protocol instead.\n\nOut of scope: squash or rebase landings, where the candidate's commits are not contained. Name the gap in the merge reference so the next actor knows it is unsolved.","body":{"branch":"request/incorporation-receipt","conditions":"1. gs merge with a candidate already contained in the checkout's target ref, all other checks passing, appends one durable receipt carrying merge_incorporation=prior, merge_head equal to merge_candidate, merge_target_pre_head equal to the observed target head, and the empty encodings for retirements, successors, left-live and changed paths; it exits 0, prints the head, and leaves the target ref, HEAD, index and working tree unchanged. 2. The fold closes the implementation commitment from that receipt under the ordinary receipt rules (satisfied, terminal landed, approved_not_landed false); a prior receipt confers no retirement authority, and a prior receipt whose plan is not empty confers no delivery either. 3. Single use: a second gs merge for the same approval is refused naming the existing receipt, and two concurrent attempts leave exactly one receipt. 4. gs merge-plan and MCP merge_plan report the incorporation mode and its reasons without signing anything. 5. Every guard has a mutation-sensitive test, and go test ./... is green at the exact head. 6. docs/reference/gs/merge.md replaces the already-contained refusal row with the incorporation behaviour and its receipt fields, names squash and rebase landings as unsolved, and docs/reference/architecture.md is updated in the same head if a layer contract moves; SKILL.md and AGENTS.md step 5 say what to do when the head already landed. 7. Independent review by codex, ratified, then landed on main and pushed.","target_head":"8e77fc25519b62b76ec89b0a2fb1311d92fa0baa","target_ref":"refs/heads/main","target_repo":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}